WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.302
Chat is the room where
your team meets the models.

2
00:00:04.302 --> 00:00:07.342
In this episode: how to
ask well, what to attach,

3
00:00:07.342 --> 00:00:11.870
how to choose a model — and how to
tell a solid answer from a fluent one.

4
00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:18.333
A chat starts in the composer, and
the composer is more than a text box.

5
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:23.668
You choose which agent answers, which
model runs, and what context rides along.

6
00:00:23.668 --> 00:00:27.180
Those three choices shape
every answer you will get.

7
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.719
Watch what happens without context.

8
00:00:30.719 --> 00:00:35.999
We ask how customers feel about our
onboarding… and the model answers — fluent,

9
00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:38.271
confident, and completely generic.

10
00:00:38.271 --> 00:00:40.477
It has never seen your customers.

11
00:00:40.477 --> 00:00:44.420
It is filling the gap with
patterns from its training data.

12
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.853
Now the same topic, grounded.

13
00:00:47.853 --> 00:00:52.397
We attach the Q2 support
review… and ask what to fix first.

14
00:00:52.397 --> 00:00:55.708
The answer changes
character: your numbers,

15
00:00:55.708 --> 00:00:59.790
your documents, named
sources you can open and check.

16
00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:07.812
This is the one habit that changes
everything about working with AI.

17
00:01:07.812 --> 00:01:14.225
Same model, same topic — the context
decides whether you get plausible words or your

18
00:01:14.225 --> 00:01:14.988
own facts.

19
00:01:14.988 --> 00:01:17.431
A fluent answer is not evidence.

20
00:01:17.431 --> 00:01:18.347
Sources are.

21
00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:21.020
When the answer matters, ground it.

22
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:25.093
The model picker is the second decision.

23
00:01:25.093 --> 00:01:29.475
Models differ — in speed, in
cost, in how deeply they reason.

24
00:01:29.475 --> 00:01:33.497
Auto lets Tale route each
request to a sensible default,

25
00:01:33.497 --> 00:01:36.442
and for most work that is the right call.

26
00:01:36.442 --> 00:01:39.890
But you can pin any
model your workspace allows.

27
00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.458
And when you want evidence
instead of opinions, run an Arena.

28
00:01:45.458 --> 00:01:48.108
One prompt, two models, side by side.

29
00:01:48.108 --> 00:01:52.047
Watch them answer the same
brief… then call the winner.

30
00:01:52.047 --> 00:01:56.344
Your verdicts flow into the
workspace analytics — over time,

31
00:01:56.344 --> 00:02:00.210
model choice becomes a
measured decision, not a taste.

32
00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.068
Big deliverables get their own workbench.

33
00:02:08.068 --> 00:02:13.951
Ask for a document — a brief, a report, a
page — and the canvas opens next to the

34
00:02:13.951 --> 00:02:14.651
chat.

35
00:02:14.651 --> 00:02:18.936
The result lands as a file you
can read, refine, and share.

36
00:02:18.936 --> 00:02:21.940
Not a wall of chat text
you copy-paste out of.

37
00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:26.809
One more thing lives here: deep research.

38
00:02:26.809 --> 00:02:29.727
The Researcher agent
plans its own searches,

39
00:02:29.727 --> 00:02:34.833
reads what it finds on the open web,
and returns a report with named sources.

40
00:02:34.833 --> 00:02:39.939
The grounding rule you just learned —
it extends all the way to the internet.

41
00:02:41.139 --> 00:02:45.586
Chat, used well: ground your
questions in your documents,

42
00:02:45.586 --> 00:02:50.267
pick models on evidence, let
big answers land in the canvas.

43
00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:55.729
Next episode: Knowledge — the
library your grounded answers come from.

44
00:02:56.929 --> 00:03:01.496
The chat guide in the
documentation covers everything you saw here,

45
00:03:01.496 --> 00:03:03.405
keyboard shortcuts included.

46
00:03:03.405 --> 00:03:05.109
See you in episode three.

